f 1045 "Classifying Quadrilaterals" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110831 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186111763 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110831 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049068 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110831 #edb/ref 1050 420.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110831 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "cceda887-69b2-551b-ab27-e3d019c77f5a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111260 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186109285 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111260 #edb/ref 1069 "q-53000" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111260 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111260 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/6b/6bffbc53c9e35da03655638ca40c5899bc5290cbadf3d14aef8e82585f14e004.png)\n\nWhich of the following terms describe the shape above?\n\nI. Kite\n\nII. Rectangle\n\nIII. Trapezoid" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111260 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "599179be-ddc2-50ab-a431-64168ebbcf12" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111260 #edb/ref 1074 "First, recall the hierarchy diagram of quadrilaterals, where each shape below another is a specific type of the one above it.\n\n![](images/1d/1dbe4c3e8ea97c0ac077c37143802366c5817d86965ab1dbc5abf54cd01d27c3.png)\n\nLet's now look at the terms one by one.\n\n- The given figure is a quadrilateral with two pairs of congruent sides that are adjacent to each other, so it is a kite.\n\n- Since the four angles of the quadrilateral are right angles, it is a rectangle.\n\n- The quadrilateral has two pairs of opposite sides that are parallel to each other, not one pair. So it is **not** a trapezoid.\n\nConsequently, the given shape is described by terms I (kite), and II (rectangle)." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111262 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186109297 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111262 #edb/ref 1069 "q-53010" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111262 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111262 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/b6/b68f8d3d0634999697051b668c06dd34c1191905a7bebda79925cd437d454d36.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding the trapezoid above?\n\nI. $\\overset{―}{AB}∥\\overset{―}{CD}.$\n\nII. $m∠ABC={90}^{∘}.$\n\nIII. $\\overset{―}{AD}∥\\overset{―}{BC}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111262 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5c9c084b-0547-549b-b2ae-ba90597387ae" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111262 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The trapezoid has a pair of parallel sides, $\\overset{―}{AB}∥\\overset{―}{CD}.$\n\n- Statement II is true. We can see that $∠ABC$ is a right angle, that is, $m∠ABC={90}^{∘}.$\n\n- Statement III is false. The trapezoid has one pair of parallel sides, $\\overset{―}{AB}∥\\overset{―}{CD}.$ We can see that $\\overset{―}{AD}$ and $\\overset{―}{BC}$ are not parallel.\n\nIn conclusion, statements I and II are true." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111598 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186111765 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111598 #edb/ref 1061 36207 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111598 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "eab37537-55a4-56e5-9fd6-7ee823f295bb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111598 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186111900 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111763 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186111598 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111763 #edb/ref 1061 9452 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111763 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "04c2712b-f67a-5317-b34e-8936b6acdd9c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111763 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186111845 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111765 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186111767 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111765 #edb/ref 1061 9454 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111765 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "4f398499-a037-5d65-8f25-ecbcfa7534c2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111765 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186110776 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111767 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186111768 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111767 #edb/ref 1061 9459 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111767 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "14f9869d-2b87-58dd-a3bd-3dfd6047425b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111767 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186110777 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111768 #edb/ref 1061 9460 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111768 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "615b803e-ca7c-56d1-9167-9829ed84b5c5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111768 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186110778 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111845 #edb/ref 1064 "There are lots of different types of quadrilaterals. Let's look at some examples.\n\n- A **trapezoid** is a quadrilateral with exactly ![](images/95/95836ae470c61ab15bfb67d6f0c92d2ad8d03d400b04473e81e58de3ee44bf32.svg) pair of opposite sides that are parallel to each other. \nThe parallel sides are called the **bases**. The other two sides are called the **legs**.\n\n![](images/9f/9fa09c46a2119d35c796065a696954931e20062fd5b3677a5484bef92bc2b0d8.png)\n\n- An **isosceles trapezoid** is a  trapezoid with congruent legs.\n\n![](images/b6/b6b2b2012c1d724f9da8b23551ec4f978f190d86875d7db7a3074635d7f40c02.png)\n\n- A **parallelogram** is a quadrilateral with ![](images/f5/f5f3224583db57b9cfe323c8489ef5e5271000002b5ea253eb98fd067a858b2d.svg) pairs of parallel sides.\n\n \n\n![](images/5d/5d0f383f3510f54b415a7ab99ef52fb30270924f97b8afec59534a96cd3edcef.png)\n\n Opposite sides and opposite angles of a parallelogram are always congruent. Moreover, if a quadrilateral has ![](images/f5/f5f3224583db57b9cfe323c8489ef5e5271000002b5ea253eb98fd067a858b2d.svg) pairs of opposite congruent sides or  ![](images/f5/f5f3224583db57b9cfe323c8489ef5e5271000002b5ea253eb98fd067a858b2d.svg) pairs of opposite congruent angles, it is always a parallelogram.\n\n![](images/39/39ccf26333785f9ef47eed68a96bc40005a6ba025fdb6e250beefe14f10b043d.png)\n\n- A **rectangle** is a parallelogram where all angles are right.\n\n \n\n![](images/55/55a16e0ba93bf1f01d576cca558805aaea9631d1fa8e43b149509d4f8eb4c834.png)\n\n A rectangle where all sides are congruent is a **square**.\n\n![](images/a0/a04b2a0e86be8b085c5e3fc906d5aa66dfea45c033d52cd4746031202a7d427d.png)\n\n- A **rhombus** is a parallelogram where all sides are congruent. The opposite angles of a rhombus are also congruent.\n\n![](images/d3/d3588d7cbe8f285198df3909df3c03fdec04f53a3f75769e46a0ab4283fa27b3.png)\n\n A rhombus where all angles are congruent is a **square**.\n\n \n\n![](images/94/941872050d5407bcd9cb532e318276adccff802230ed854a2d292666fc0096fc.png)\n\n- A **kite** is a quadrilateral with two pairs of congruent sides that are adjacent to each other.\n\n![](images/11/116e323d81ee190c0f69b4d7cdcefe9f0952da883b0d6d3c6d4036119794baa7.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111845 #edb/ref 1065 2262 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111845 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "06a11fa2-5911-52f5-9a6d-1028e49b44dd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111845 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111900 #edb/ref 1064 "We can organize all mentioned types of quadrilaterals into the following hierarchy diagram:\n\n![](images/1d/1dbe4c3e8ea97c0ac077c37143802366c5817d86965ab1dbc5abf54cd01d27c3.png)\n\nEach shape below another is a specific type of the one above it. For example, every square is a rectangle, and every rectangle is a parallelogram. This means every square is also a parallelogram." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111900 #edb/ref 1065 5931 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111900 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "d9d3be2f-753b-5a4b-9f26-20b34ab8f89a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111900 #edb/ref 1067 "Classifying Quadrilaterals" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446819 #edb/ref 1080 "I and III only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446819 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446819 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "08dad930-3bec-5023-9f09-d5e37c38df03" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446820 #edb/ref 1080 "II and III only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446820 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446820 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "274c9f91-07ea