gram of quadrilaterals, where each shape below another is a specific type of the one above it.\n\n![](images/1d/1dbe4c3e8ea97c0ac077c37143802366c5817d86965ab1dbc5abf54cd01d27c3.png)\n\nLet's now look at the terms one by one.\n\n- The given figure is a quadrilateral with angles that are not all right. So it is **not** a square.\n\n- Since the four sides of the quadrilateral are congruent, it is a rhombus.\n\n- The quadrilateral is a rhombus. A rhombus is a parallelogram where all four sides are equal. So it is a parallelogram.\n\nConsequently, the given shape is described by terms II (rhombus), and III (parallelogram)." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446850 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186446847 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446850 #edb/ref 1069 "q-53060" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446850 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446850 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. A square is always a kite.\n\nII. A rectangle is always a square.\n\nIII. A rhombus is always a trapezoid." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446850 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8fe04944-0102-5a76-9a34-b22553e0ce91" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446850 #edb/ref 1074 "First, recall the hierarchy diagram of quadrilaterals, where each shape below another is a specific type of the one above it.\n\n![](images/1d/1dbe4c3e8ea97c0ac077c37143802366c5817d86965ab1dbc5abf54cd01d27c3.png)\n\nLet's now look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. A kite has two pairs of congruent sides that are adjacent to each other. A square fits the description because all the sides in a square are congruent.\n\n- Statement II is false. In a square, all the sides are congruent. However, in a rectangle, it's only necessary that two pairs of opposite sides are congruent to each other. Therefore, a rectangle is not always a square.\n\n- Statement III is false. In a trapezoid, only $1$ pair of opposite sides are parallel to each other. However, in a rhombus, the $2$ pairs of opposite sides are parallel to each other. Therefore, a rhombus is not a trapezoid.\n\nIn conclusion, only statement I is true." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446851 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186446844 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446851 #edb/ref 1069 "q-128291" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446851 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446851 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/94/94a6607f6d12c3c59c9822d91dfb6c6b8b3a8d23bba9ef1c3a7253be564f95d8.png)\n\nWhich of the figures above is a parallelogram?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446851 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d149b00e-3076-56e3-b5b0-ca0e159fc928" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446851 #edb/ref 1074 "First, recall the hierarchy diagram of quadrilaterals, where each shape below another is a specific type of the one above it.\n\n![](images/1d/1dbe4c3e8ea97c0ac077c37143802366c5817d86965ab1dbc5abf54cd01d27c3.png)\n\nRemember that a parallelogram is a quadrilateral with $2$ pairs of parallel sides.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each shape in turn.\n\n- The shape $P$ is an isosceles trapezoid. There is a pair of opposite sides that are not parallel, therefore, it is not a parallelogram.\n\n- The shape $Q$ is a square. Therefore, its opposite sides are parallel, so it is a parallelogram.\n\n- The shape $R$ is a rhombus. Since every pair of opposite sides in a rhombus are parallel, it is a parallelogram.\n\n- The shape $S$ is a kite in which no pair of opposite sides are parallel, so it is not a parallelogram.\n\nTherefore, shapes $Q$ and $R$ are parallelograms. The shapes $P$ and $S$ are not parallelograms." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446852 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186446848 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446852 #edb/ref 1069 "q-128251" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446852 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446852 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/78/781e1e9f5a08cf7f01d9896e957f8d983cd428817e3a5eeb90312a89fdc0a932.png)\n\nWhich of the figures above is a rhombus?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446852 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e6a1177b-8e6f-5855-8414-02d987aa4107" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446852 #edb/ref 1074 "First, recall the hierarchy diagram of quadrilaterals, where each shape below another is a specific type of the one above it.\n\n![](images/1d/1dbe4c3e8ea97c0ac077c37143802366c5817d86965ab1dbc5abf54cd01d27c3.png)\n\nRemember that a  rhombus is a parallelogram where all sides are congruent. The opposite angles of a rhombus are also congruent.  \n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each shape in turn.\n\n- The shape $P$ is a rectangle. The sides are not all congruent, so it is not a rhombus.\n\n- The shape $Q$ is a kite. Since the sides are not all congruent, it is not a rhombus.\n\n- The shape $R$ is a rhombus since all its sides are congruent and the opposite sides are parallel.\n\n- The shape $S$ is an isosceles trapezoid.  Since it has only one pair of congruent sides, it is not a rhombus.\n\nTherefore, only shape $R$ is a rhombus." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446853 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186446845 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446853 #edb/ref 1069 "q-128157" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446853 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446853 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/da/da2336d6c4360d10fedbfc6441280b5a08091df34f3f59e2e94e7d4daf01bc3f.png)\n\nWhich of the following terms describe the shape above?\n\nI. Square\n\nII. Trapezoid\n\nIII. Rhombus" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446853 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ef405c5c-f229-5ca4-acda-70f35ff263e9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446853 #edb/ref 1074 "First, recall the hierarchy diagram of quadrilaterals, where each shape below another is a specific type of the one above it.\n\n![](images/1d/1dbe4c3e8ea97c0ac077c37143802366c5817d86965ab1dbc5abf54cd01d27c3.png)\n\nLet's now look at the terms one by one.\n\n- The given figure is a quadrilateral where all sides are congruent and all angles are right, so it is a square.\n\n- Since the quadrilateral has two pairs of opposite sides that are parallel to each other, not one pair, it is **not** a trapezoid.\n\n- The quadrilateral is a parallelogram where all sides are congruent. So it is a rhombus.\n\nConsequently, the given shape is described by terms I (square)  and III (rhombus)." #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
